.TH PODUP 1 "July 2026" "podup 3.3.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
podup \- run Compose projects on rootless Podman
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B podup
[\fIGLOBAL OPTIONS\fR]
\fICOMMAND\fR
[\fICOMMAND OPTIONS\fR]
[\fISERVICE\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B podup
reads a Compose file (\fBcompose.yaml\fR or the rest of the compose-spec
precedence list) and translates it into rootless Podman operations over Podman's native
libpod REST API. It is a single static binary with no daemon and no Python
runtime, usable both as a standalone Compose replacement and as a Rust
library.
.PP
.B podup
requires
.B Podman 5.0
or newer: it targets the
.B /v5.0.0/libpod
API surface. The socket must be local \(em a
.B unix://
path (or an
.B npipe://
pipe on Windows); remote
.B tcp://
or
.B ssh://
endpoints are rejected.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-file " " \fIPATH\fR
Path to the compose file. May be given multiple times to merge files in order;
later files override earlier ones. May also be set via \fBCOMPOSE_FILE\fR. When
unset, the compose-spec precedence list is probed in the current directory
(\fBcompose.yaml\fR, \fBcompose.yml\fR, \fBdocker-compose.yaml\fR,
\fBdocker-compose.yml\fR).
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-project " " \fINAME\fR
Project name (also spelled \fB\-\-project\-name\fR), used as a prefix for container, network and volume names. May
also be set via \fBCOMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME\fR. When unset, the top-level
\fBname:\fR field is used, then the sanitized basename of the project directory.
.TP
.BR \-\-socket " " \fIPATH\fR
Podman socket path. Overrides auto-detection and \fBPODMAN_SOCKET\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-profile " " \fINAMES\fR
Active profiles, comma-separated. May also be set via \fBCOMPOSE_PROFILES\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-project\-directory " " \fIPATH\fR
Base directory for resolving relative paths (env_file, build context, bind
mounts, config and secret sources). Defaults to the compose file's directory.
.TP
.BR \-\-connection\-pool\-size " " \fIN\fR
Maximum number of HTTP/1.1 connections the libpod client keeps open to the
Podman socket for reuse, from 0 to 1024. Default 0 means no pool. May also be
set via \fBPODUP_LIBPOD_POOL\fR; the older misspelling \fBPODUP_LIBCOD_POOL\fR
is still read when the new name is unset.
.TP
.BR \-\-ansi " " \fIWHEN\fR
When to colour output: \fIauto\fR (terminals only, and \fBNO_COLOR\fR forces
plain output), \fIalways\fR or \fInever\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-no\-warn
Suppress the host-binding and privilege-escalation warnings printed during
\fBup\fR, \fBcreate\fR, \fBrun\fR and \fBexec\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-env\-file " " \fIPATH\fR
Additional env file loaded into the variable map used for interpolation. May be
given multiple times; later files win. The process environment and a project
\fB.env\fR still take precedence.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B up
Create and start all services. Options: \fB\-d\fR/\fB\-\-detach\fR,
\fB\-\-build\fR, \fB\-w\fR/\fB\-\-watch\fR, \fB\-\-remove\-orphans\fR,
\fB\-\-no\-recreate\fR, \fB\-\-force\-recreate\fR, \fB\-\-no\-deps\fR,
\fB\-\-scale\fR \fISERVICE=N\fR (repeatable), \fB\-\-pull\fR \fIpolicy\fR,
\fB\-\-no\-build\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\-pull\fR, \fB\-\-wait\fR,
\fB\-\-wait\-timeout\fR \fISECONDS\fR, \fB\-t\fR/\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fISECONDS\fR,
\fB\-V\fR/\fB\-\-renew\-anon\-volumes\fR, \fB\-\-timestamps\fR,
\fB\-\-no\-start\fR, \fB\-\-abort\-on\-container\-exit\fR,
\fB\-\-exit\-code\-from\fR \fISERVICE\fR (implies the previous one),
\fB\-\-abort\-on\-container\-failure\fR (stops only on a non-zero exit). These
three cannot be combined with \fB\-d\fR, \fB\-\-wait\fR or \fB\-\-watch\fR.
Output: \fB\-\-no\-color\fR, \fB\-\-no\-log\-prefix\fR,
\fB\-\-quiet\-build\fR, \fB\-\-attach\fR \fISERVICE\fR and
\fB\-\-no\-attach\fR \fISERVICE\fR (both repeatable; \fB\-\-attach\fR cannot be
combined with \fB\-\-attach\-dependencies\fR, which also follows the logs of the
dependencies of the named services; by default only the named services are
followed). \fB\-\-always\-recreate\-deps\fR
recreates the services that were not named, and cannot be combined with
\fB\-\-no\-recreate\fR. \fB\-y\fR/\fB\-\-yes\fR is accepted and changes nothing,
because \fBup\fR never asks for confirmation. Accepts a
trailing list of services to bring up (with their transitive depends_on).
.TP
.B scale \fISERVICE=N\fR...
Set the number of running containers for services, creating missing replicas and
removing surplus ones. A service publishing a fixed host port cannot scale past
one replica and the command fails with guidance.
.TP
.B create
Create the containers for services without starting them. Options:
\fB\-\-build\fR, \fB\-\-force\-recreate\fR, \fB\-\-no\-recreate\fR,
\fB\-\-no\-deps\fR, \fB\-\-pull\fR \fIpolicy\fR, \fB\-\-no\-build\fR (not with
\fB\-\-build\fR), \fB\-\-scale\fR \fISERVICE=N\fR (repeatable),
\fB\-\-remove\-orphans\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\-pull\fR, and a trailing service list.
.TP
.B ls
List podup compose projects on the host. \fB\-a\fR/\fB\-\-all\fR includes stopped
projects, \fB\-q\fR/\fB\-\-quiet\fR prints names only, \fB\-\-format\fR selects
\fItable\fR or \fIjson\fR, and \fB\-\-filter\fR \fIname=NAME\fR or
\fIstatus=running|exited\fR is repeatable. Requires no compose file.
.TP
.B down \fR[\fISERVICE\fR...]
Stop and remove containers. With service names only those services are removed,
and the networks, the pod and the volumes are left alone; \fB\-v\fR cannot be
combined with names. \fB\-v\fR/\fB\-\-volumes\fR also removes named
volumes declared in the compose file; \fB\-\-remove\-orphans\fR also removes
containers for services no longer in the file; both are refused when the
project was created from another directory unless \fB\-\-force\fR is given;
\fB\-\-rmi\fR \fIall\fR|\fIlocal\fR also removes the service images; \fB\-t\fR/\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fISECONDS\fR is the
grace period before containers are killed.
.TP
.B start
Start existing stopped containers. \fB\-\-wait\fR waits until services are
running or healthy, bounded by \fB\-\-wait\-timeout\fR \fISECONDS\fR.
.TP
.B stop
Stop running containers without removing them. \fB\-t\fR/\fB\-\-timeout\fR
\fISECONDS\fR is the grace period before containers are killed.
.TP
.B build
Build or rebuild service images. Options: \fB\-\-no\-cache\fR, \fB\-\-pull\fR,
\fB\-\-build\-arg\fR \fIKEY=VAL\fR (repeatable), \fB\-\-progress\fR \fIstyle\fR
(validated, inert), \fB\-\-push\fR, \fB\-q\fR/\fB\-\-quiet\fR,
\fB\-\-with\-dependencies\fR (also build what the named services depend on).
.TP
.B push
Push each service's image to its registry (imageless services are skipped).
Credentials come from \fBpodman login\fR. \fB\-\-ignore\-push\-failures\fR
continues after a failure; \fB\-q\fR/\fB\-\-quiet\fR suppresses progress; \fB\-\-tls\-verify false\fR allows an insecure
registry.
.TP
.B rm
Remove stopped service containers. \fB\-f\fR/\fB\-\-force\fR removes running
containers (stopping them first); \fB\-v\fR/\fB\-\-volumes\fR also removes
anonymous volumes attached to them; \fB\-s\fR/\fB\-\-stop\fR stops them first.
.TP
.B kill
Send a signal to service containers. \fB\-s\fR/\fB\-\-signal\fR sets the signal
(default \fBSIGKILL\fR); \fB\-\-remove\-orphans\fR removes containers for services
no longer in the file.
.TP
.B pause
Pause running service containers.
.TP
.B unpause
Resume paused service containers.
.TP
.B run \fISERVICE\fR [\fICOMMAND\fR...]
Run a one-off command in a new service container. Options: \fB\-\-rm\fR
(default true), \fB\-\-no\-rm\fR, \fB\-d\fR/\fB\-\-detach\fR,
\fB\-e\fR/\fB\-\-env\fR KEY=VAL, \fB\-\-env\-from\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR (repeatable),
\fB\-\-name\fR, \fB\-P\fR/\fB\-\-service\-ports\fR,
\fB\-u\fR/\fB\-\-user\fR, \fB\-w\fR/\fB\-\-workdir\fR, \fB\-\-entrypoint\fR,
\fB\-v\fR/\fB\-\-volume\fR (repeatable), \fB\-p\fR/\fB\-\-publish\fR (repeatable),
\fB\-l\fR/\fB\-\-label\fR KEY=VAL (repeatable),
\fB\-i\fR/\fB\-\-interactive\fR, \fB\-T\fR/\fB\-\-no\-TTY\fR, \fB\-\-no\-deps\fR,
\fB\-\-remove\-orphans\fR (removes the containers of services no longer in the file first).
A pseudo-TTY with live stdin is allocated when both stdin and stdout are
terminals (\fB\-T\fR opts out, \fB\-d\fR never allocates one), so
\fBpodup run -it app sh\fR is an interactive session on Linux, macOS and Windows.
.TP
.B cp \fISRC\fR \fIDST\fR
Copy files between a service container and the host. Use \fBSERVICE:PATH\fR for
the container side. \fB\-\-index\fR selects a replica (1-based) of a scaled
service, \fB\-L\fR/\fB\-\-follow\-link\fR follows symlinks in the host source, and
\fB\-a\fR/\fB\-\-archive\fR is accepted for compatibility and has no effect.
.TP
.B attach \fISERVICE\fR
Attach to a service container's output (stdout/stderr), streaming it until the
container exits. \fB\-\-index\fR selects a replica (1-based);
\fB\-\-no\-stdin\fR, \fB\-\-sig\-proxy\fR and \fB\-\-detach\-keys\fR are accepted for
compatibility.
.TP
.B wait \fR[\fISERVICE\fR...]
Block until the named service containers (default: all) stop, printing each
container's exit code. \fB\-\-format json\fR emits one JSON object per
container as it exits.
.TP
.B commit \fISERVICE\fR \fIIMAGE\fR
Commit a service container to a new image reference (\fBrepo[:tag]\fR).
\fB\-\-index\fR selects a replica (1-based) of a scaled service,
\fB\-m\fR/\fB\-\-message\fR, \fB\-a\fR/\fB\-\-author\fR and \fB\-c\fR/\fB\-\-change\fR
\fIINSTRUCTION\fR (repeatable) set image metadata, and \fB\-p\fR/\fB\-\-pause\fR
pauses the container during the commit (default on).
.TP
.B export \fISERVICE\fR
Export a service container's filesystem as a tar archive. \fB\-o\fR/\fB\-\-output\fR
writes to a file instead of stdout; \fB\-\-index\fR selects a replica (1-based).
.TP
.B ps
List project containers. \fB\-a\fR/\fB\-\-all\fR includes stopped ones,
\fB\-q\fR/\fB\-\-quiet\fR prints IDs only, \fB\-s\fR/\fB\-\-size\fR adds each
container's on-disk size, \fB\-\-format\fR selects \fItable\fR, \fIjson\fR or
\fIndjson\fR, \fB\-\-services\fR prints service names, \fB\-\-all\-services\fR
prints every configured service name including those without containers, and
\fB\-\-filter\fR \fIstatus=...\fR or \fIname=...\fR and \fB\-\-status\fR \fISTATUS\fR
are repeatable.
.TP
.B top
Display the running processes of service containers. \fB\-\-format\fR selects
\fItable\fR or \fIjson\fR.
.TP
.B stats
Live resource usage (CPU, memory, network, block I/O, PIDs) for service
containers. \fB\-\-no\-stream\fR prints a single snapshot; \fB\-a\fR/\fB\-\-all\fR
includes non-running containers, \fB\-\-no\-trunc\fR keeps long names whole,
\fB\-\-format\fR selects \fItable\fR or \fIjson\fR; a trailing service list
narrows the report.
.TP
.B port \fISERVICE\fR \fIPRIVATE_PORT\fR
Print the public binding for a container port. \fB\-\-proto\fR sets the protocol
(\fB\-\-protocol\fR is an alias; default \fBtcp\fR) and \fB\-\-index\fR selects a replica (1-based).
.TP
.B events
Stream Podman events for this project's containers.
\fB\-\-format\fR \fItable\fR|\fIjson\fR selects the output shape (json emits one
object per line, not an array); \fB\-\-json\fR is a deprecated alias for it.
\fB\-\-filter\fR \fIKEY=VALUE\fR keeps only matching events and is repeatable.
\fB\-\-since\fR and \fB\-\-until\fR bound the window and must be given
together to end the feed \- either alone, or an \fB\-\-until\fR in the future,
leaves it following indefinitely.
.TP
.B images
List images used by services. \fB\-q\fR/\fB\-\-quiet\fR prints image IDs only,
\fB\-\-format\fR selects \fItable\fR or \fIjson\fR.
.TP
.B volumes \fR[\fISERVICE\fR...]
List the project's named volumes. \fB\-q\fR/\fB\-\-quiet\fR prints names only,
\fB\-\-format\fR selects \fItable\fR or \fIjson\fR, and a trailing service list
narrows the report to volumes those services mount. \fB\-s\fR/\fB\-\-size\fR adds
each volume's size (slow).
.TP
.B logs \fR[\fISERVICE\fR]
View container output. \fB\-f\fR/\fB\-\-follow\fR streams new output,
\fB\-n\fR/\fB\-\-tail\fR \fIN\fR limits to the last N lines, \fB\-\-since\fR and
\fB\-\-until\fR bound by time, and \fB\-t\fR/\fB\-\-timestamps\fR prefixes each
line with an RFC3339 timestamp. \fB\-\-no\-color\fR drops the colour from the
service-name prefix and \fB\-\-no\-log\-prefix\fR drops the prefix.
\fB\-\-index\fR \fIN\fR shows only replica N (1-based) of a scaled service.
.TP
.B exec \fISERVICE\fR \fICOMMAND\fR...
Execute a command in a running service container. Options:
\fB\-e\fR/\fB\-\-env\fR KEY=VAL (repeatable), \fB\-u\fR/\fB\-\-user\fR,
\fB\-w\fR/\fB\-\-workdir\fR, \fB\-\-privileged\fR, \fB\-d\fR/\fB\-\-detach\fR,
\fB\-T\fR/\fB\-\-no\-tty\fR, \fB\-\-index\fR \fIN\fR. A pseudo-TTY
with live stdin is allocated by default
when both stdin and stdout are terminals (\fB\-T\fR opts out), on Linux, macOS
and Windows.
.TP
.B pull
Pull images for all services. \fB\-q\fR/\fB\-\-quiet\fR suppresses progress
output, \fB\-\-policy\fR \fIpolicy\fR (always, missing, never, newer, build)
overrides each service's \fBpull_policy\fR, \fB\-\-ignore\-pull\-failures\fR
continues after a failure, \fB\-\-include\-deps\fR also pulls the
\fBdepends_on\fR services of the named ones and \fB\-\-ignore\-buildable\fR
skips services that declare a \fBbuild:\fR section.
.TP
.B restart \fR[\fISERVICE\fR]
Restart services, or one named service. \fB\-\-no\-deps\fR skips
cascade-restarting dependents with a \fBdepends_on\fR restart condition;
\fB\-t\fR/\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fISECONDS\fR is the grace period before containers are
killed.
.TP
.B config
Print the resolved compose file after substitution, extends and include.
\fB\-\-format\fR selects \fIyaml\fR or \fIjson\fR, \fB\-\-services\fR lists the
service names, \fB\-q\fR/\fB\-\-quiet\fR only validates (no output),
\fB\-\-no\-interpolate\fR leaves \fB${VAR}\fR placeholders literal, and
\fB\-\-resolve\-image\-digests\fR rewrites each service \fBimage:\fR to its
registry digest (\fBrepo@sha256:...\fR). \fB\-\-volumes\fR, \fB\-\-networks\fR, \fB\-\-images\fR and
\fB\-\-profiles\fR list the named volumes, networks, image references or profile names,
\fB\-\-hash\fR \fIS1,S2\fR (or \fB*\fR) prints the config hash of services, and
\fB\-\-no\-normalize\fR is accepted for compatibility.
.TP
.B audit
Audit the compose file for hardening gaps (writable root filesystem, kept
capabilities, missing memory and PID limits, wildcard host binds, secret-shaped
environment variables, unpinned images and similar) and print one row per
service. Read-only: it never contacts Podman and changes nothing that \fBup\fR
does. \fB\-\-format\fR selects \fItable\fR or \fIjson\fR, \fB\-\-strict\fR exits 1
when any finding is present so it can fail CI, \fB\-\-wildcard\-binds\fR enables
the opt-in \fBport_published_on_wildcard\fR check, and \fB\-\-list\-checks\fR
prints the checks this build carries and exits without reading a compose file
(it conflicts with \fB\-\-strict\fR and \fB\-\-wildcard\-binds\fR).
.TP
.B generate quadlet \fR[\fB\-o\fR \fIDIR\fR]
Translate the compose file into Podman Quadlet unit files (one \fB.container\fR
per service plus \fB.network\fR and \fB.volume\fR units). Without
\fB\-o\fR/\fB\-\-output\fR the units are printed to stdout.
.TP
.B watch
Watch for file changes and sync, rebuild or restart services per the
\fBdevelop.watch\fR rules.
.TP
.B update
Update podup to the latest signed release. Verifies the release's Ed25519
signature against the embedded public key and its SHA-256 checksum, failing
closed. \fB\-\-check\fR only reports whether a newer release exists;
\fB\-\-force\fR reinstalls even when not newer.
.TP
.B autostart \fISUBCOMMAND\fR
Manage a boot-time autostart unit for this project, rootless and user-scope via
\fBsystemctl \-\-user\fR (units under \fB${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-~/.config}\fR).
Subcommands: \fBinstall\fR (\fB\-\-mode\fR \fIservice\fR|\fIquadlet\fR|\fIstart\fR,
\fB\-\-no\-start\fR, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR, and \fB\-\-auto\-update\fR
\fIhourly\fR|\fIdaily\fR|\fIweekly\fR, which installs a timer that runs
\fBpodup up \-d\fR on that schedule and is accepted only with
\fB\-\-mode service\fR), \fBuninstall\fR (\fB\-\-purge\fR also tears the stack
down and removes its named volumes), \fBstatus\fR, and \fBrebuild\fR (for a
quadlet-mode install).
.TP
.B version
Print podup's version. \fB\-\-short\fR prints just the number;
\fB\-\-format json\fR emits it as JSON.
.TP
.B completions \fISHELL\fR
Print a shell completion script to stdout for \fBbash\fR, \fBzsh\fR, \fBfish\fR,
\fBpowershell\fR or \fBelvish\fR. The Debian package installs the bash, zsh and
fish scripts automatically.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B COMPOSE_FILE
Path-separator-delimited list of compose files (see \fB\-\-file\fR).
Like the next three variables, it can also be set in the project's \fB.env\fR.
.TP
.B COMPOSE_PATH_SEPARATOR
Separator of the \fBCOMPOSE_FILE\fR list; \fB:\fR (\fB;\fR on Windows) when unset.
.TP
.B COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME
Default project name (see \fB\-\-project\fR).
.TP
.B COMPOSE_PROFILES
Default active profiles (see \fB\-\-profile\fR).
.TP
.B COMPOSE_ENV_FILES
Comma-separated env files, the same as repeating \fB\-\-env\-file\fR. Read from
the process environment only.
.TP
.B PODUP_LIBPOD_POOL
Size of the libpod connection pool (see \fB\-\-connection\-pool\-size\fR).
\fBPODUP_LIBCOD_POOL\fR, a misspelling of the name, is read when this one is
unset.
.TP
.B PODMAN_SOCKET
Podman socket path (see \fB\-\-socket\fR).
.TP
.B DOCKER_HOST
Docker-compatible fallback for the Podman socket, used only when
\fBPODMAN_SOCKET\fR is unset. Must name a local \fBunix://\fR socket
(or \fBnpipe://\fR on Windows); a remote \fBtcp://\fR or \fBssh://\fR value is
rejected.
.TP
.B RUST_LOG
Log verbosity filter. With no value set, warnings and errors are shown; set
e.g. \fBRUST_LOG=podup=info\fR or \fBRUST_LOG=podup=debug\fR for more detail.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
A command failed (for example a parse error or a Podman error).
.TP
.B 2
A usage error (invalid arguments or flags).
.TP
.B 3
\fBupdate\fR failed to verify or install a release.
.TP
.B 126
\fBrun\fR/\fBexec\fR: the command exists but is not executable.
.TP
.B 127
\fBrun\fR/\fBexec\fR: the command was not found.
.TP
.B 130
An attached \fBup\fR was ended by SIGINT or SIGTERM. 130 for both;
the project is still torn down before the code is
returned.
.PP
\fBrun\fR propagates the container's own exit code verbatim, so any other
non-zero status comes from the container that was run.
.PP
A streaming command whose connection dies exits 1 rather than reporting success.
This covers \fBlogs\fR, \fBstats\fR, an attached \fBup\fR, \fBrun\fR and
\fBevents\fR. A stream that ends because its container stopped still exits 0.
For \fBevents\fR, an unbounded feed that ends at all exits 1; bounding one
needs \fB--since\fR and \fB--until\fR together, both already elapsed.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B compose.yaml
Default compose file when none is given. The compose-spec precedence list is
probed in the current directory: \fBcompose.yaml\fR, \fBcompose.yml\fR,
\fBdocker-compose.yaml\fR, then \fBdocker-compose.yml\fR. When the file is
found this way, and not named with \fB\-f\fR or \fBCOMPOSE_FILE\fR, the first
override file next to it is merged on top: \fBcompose.override.yaml\fR,
\fBcompose.override.yml\fR, \fBdocker-compose.override.yaml\fR or
\fBdocker-compose.override.yml\fR.
.TP
.B .env
Project environment file, loaded for variable interpolation.
.SH SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
.B podup
runs entirely as the invoking user against a rootless Podman socket; it adds no
privilege of its own and holds no setuid bit. It talks to Podman over the
libpod REST API on a Unix socket
.RB ( PODMAN_SOCKET ),
which is the trust boundary: any user who can reach that socket already controls
the container engine.
.PP
Compose files are treated as
.B trusted input
\(em like a Makefile. Path-valued keys
.RB ( extends.file ", " env_file ", " include )
may reference files outside the project directory, so do not run
.B podup
on a compose file from an untrusted source.
.PP
.B podup update
and the installer verify every downloaded release against an embedded Ed25519
public key (and a GitHub build-provenance attestation) before installing,
failing closed if no verifier is available; there is no bypass. The Debian
package omits the self-update path and relies on
.BR apt (8)
for signed upgrades.
.PP
Running with
.B RUST_LOG=debug
can emit environment variable values and resolved secret/config file paths to
the log; avoid debug logging where the terminal or log sink is not trusted.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR podman (1),
.BR podman-systemd.unit (5)
.PP
Full documentation at: \fIhttps://github.com/Glyndor/podup\fR
.SH BUGS
Report bugs at \fIhttps://github.com/Glyndor/podup/issues\fR.
